From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Darren Shepherd <darren@obot.ai>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 12:19:14 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] renderer: d3d11 the colour atlas is sRGB, so emoji are not
 washed out

`cell_text` takes its colour-glyph sample as linear - "Color glyphs are
already premultiplied linear" - and unlinearizes only when blending is
not linear. Both other backends arrange that by asking the hardware for
the decode: Metal creates the bgra atlas as `bgra8unorm_srgb`
(Metal.zig:374) and OpenGL as `srgba` (OpenGL.zig:430), in each case for
the colour atlas only, since grayscale holds coverage rather than colour.

This backend created both atlases as plain UNORM. So sRGB-encoded emoji
were read as if they were already linear and then encoded again on the
way out, which lifts every midtone: reported from use as "washed out and
flat", and it was. Sampled off a capture of the same glyph, the grinning
face's fill went #FEF178 with #8B7240 features - a pale yellow and a
muddy brown - where the font's own colours are #FDE030 and #422B0D.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/renderer/D3D11.zig | 21 ++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/renderer/D3D11.zig b/src/renderer/D3D11.zig
index bcd50cfa6..f86fcd1ae 100644
--- a/src/renderer/D3D11.zig
+++ b/src/renderer/D3D11.zig
@@ -494,9 +494,24 @@ pub fn initAtlasTexture(
             .device = self.device.device,
             .context = self.device.context,
             .format = format,
-            // The glyph atlas holds coverage/color data that the shader
-            // samples directly; it must not be sRGB-decoded on read.
-            .srgb = false,
+            // The two atlases hold different kinds of data, and only one of
+            // them is colour.
+            //
+            // Grayscale is coverage - not a colour at all - so decoding it as
+            // sRGB would be meaningless. Colour glyphs are sRGB-encoded BGRA
+            // out of the rasterizer, and `cell_text` takes its sample as
+            // *linear* ("Color glyphs are already premultiplied linear"),
+            // unlinearizing it again only when blending is not linear. The
+            // hardware does that decode for free when the view is sRGB, which
+            // is how both other backends get it: Metal asks for
+            // `bgra8unorm_srgb` and OpenGL for `srgba`, in each case for the
+            // bgra atlas only.
+            //
+            // Without it, sRGB values are read as if they were linear and then
+            // encoded on the way out, which lifts every midtone: emoji came
+            // out pale and desaturated - a washed-out yellow face where the
+            // font draws a saturated orange one.
+            .srgb = format == .bgra,
         },
         atlas.size,
         atlas.size,
